body:grpc {
  name: message 1
  content: '''
    {
      "texts": [
        "3.5g",
        "1,000 mg",
        "1/8 oz",
        "1 1/2 oz",
        "½ oz",
        "18-22%",
        "18 to 22 %",
        "25 mg/5 ml",
        "2 pack"
      ]
    }
  '''
}

docs {
  `recordsimilarity.v1.Quantities/Parse` reads amounts written as text into quantities, one answer for each text, in
  order. For a caller whose source holds an amount as a string: a record takes a number and a unit and never a text,
  so such a caller asks here first and puts what it gets into the record. The whole text is the amount, or it is not
  an amount: nothing is looked for inside prose, and nothing is guessed. It reads no record, runs no model, and looks
  at no schema or limit: the answer comes from the request alone, on a server in any state, and the same request
  always gets the same answer.
  
  ## This request
  
  Nine texts, one in each form that is read. All nine are amounts:
  
  | Text | `quantity` | `dimension` | `normalized` |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `3.5g` | 3.5 `g` | `DIMENSION_MASS` | 3500 `mg` |
  | `1,000 mg` | 1000 `mg` | `DIMENSION_MASS` | 1000 `mg` |
  | `1/8 oz` | 0.125 `oz` | `DIMENSION_MASS` | 3543.690390625 `mg` |
  | `1 1/2 oz` | 1.5 `oz` | `DIMENSION_MASS` | 42524.284687499996 `mg` |
  | `½ oz` | 0.5 `oz` | `DIMENSION_MASS` | 14174.7615625 `mg` |
  | `18-22%` | 18 to 22 `%` | `DIMENSION_FRACTION` | 18 to 22 `%` |
  | `18 to 22 %` | 18 to 22 `%` | `DIMENSION_FRACTION` | 18 to 22 `%` |
  | `25 mg/5 ml` | 25 `mg/5 ml` | `DIMENSION_OTHER` | 25 `mg/5 ml` |
  | `2 pack` | 2 `pack` | `DIMENSION_OTHER` | 2 `pack` |
  
  The first answer as it is sent, and the others have the same shape:
  
  ```json
  {
    "quantity": { "min": 3.5, "max": 3.5, "unit": "g" },
    "dimension": "DIMENSION_MASS",
    "normalized": { "min": 3500.0, "max": 3500.0, "unit": "mg" }
  }
  ```
  
  A comma only groups thousands. `25 mg/5 ml` and `2 pack` are amounts in units the server does not convert: not a
  refusal, and `DIMENSION_OTHER` says so.
  
  ## Request (`ParseAmountsRequest`)
  
  | Field | Type | Required | Values |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `texts` | list of string | no | The texts to read, each the whole of one amount. At most 1000 in one call; an empty list gets an empty answer. The space around a text, and runs of it inside, are not part of it. |
  
  What a text may be, and still be read:
  
  | Form | Examples | Read as |
  |---|---|---|
  | a number and a unit | `3.5 g`, `3.5g`, `.5 g`, `22%`, `12 fl oz` | that number, in that unit |
  | thousands grouped by commas | `1,000 mg`, `1,000.5 mg` | 1000, 1000.5 |
  | a fraction | `1/8 oz`, `3/2 oz`, `⅛ oz`, `½ oz` | 0.125, 1.5, 0.125, 0.5 |
  | a mixed number | `1 1/2 oz`, `1-1/2 oz`, `1½ oz` | 1.5 |
  | a range | `18-22%`, `18 to 22 %`, `18% - 22%`, `1/8 - 1/4 oz` | `min` and `max`, in the unit the last end says |
  | a unit the server does not convert | `2 pack`, `25 mg/5 ml`, `5mg per serving` | the number, with that unit as written: `DIMENSION_OTHER` |
  | a weight named by a word | `gram`, `half gram`, `eighth`, `quarter`, `half ounce`, `ounce`, `zip` | the weight it is sold as, in grams (below) |
  
  The words, with every spelling that is read. A word is read only when it is the whole text, in any case:
  
  | Words | Read as |
  |---|---|
  | `gram`, `a gram` | 1 g |
  | `half gram`, `half a gram`, `a half gram`, `half g` | 0.5 g |
  | `eighth`, `an eighth`, `eighth oz`, `eighth ounce` | 3.5 g |
  | `quarter`, `a quarter`, `quarter oz`, `quarter ounce` | 7 g |
  | `half ounce`, `half oz`, `half an ounce`, `a half ounce` | 14 g |
  | `ounce`, `an ounce`, `zip`, `a zip` | 28 g |
  
  A number and a unit stay exact: `1/8 oz` is 0.125 oz, which is 3.54 g, where `eighth` is 3.5 g.
  
  ## Response (`ParseAmountsResponse`)
  
  | Field | Type | Meaning |
  |---|---|---|
  | `amounts` | list of `ParsedAmount` | One for each text of the request, in the request's order. |
  
  ### `amounts[]` (`ParsedAmount`)
  
  | Field | Type | Meaning |
  |---|---|---|
  | `quantity` | `Quantity`, may be absent | The amount, when the text is one: usable as a record's quantity as it is. A single value has `min` equal to `max`. The unit is as the text wrote it, with single spaces. Absent when the text is not an amount. |
  | `problem` | `AmountProblem` | Why the text is not an amount. One of the values below. `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_UNSPECIFIED`, which JSON leaves out, when `quantity` is present. |
  | `dimension` | `Dimension` | What the amount's unit measures. `DIMENSION_OTHER` says the unit is one the server does not convert, which is worth a look before it becomes a record's baseline. `DIMENSION_UNSPECIFIED` when `quantity` is absent. |
  | `normalized` | `Quantity`, may be absent | The same amount in the one unit its dimension is compared in: `mg` for a mass, `ml` for a volume, `%` for a share. It is what `Convert` answers for `quantity` with no `to_unit`. For a unit the server does not convert, the amount is unchanged and the unit is in lower case with single spaces. Absent when `quantity` is. Either may be sent as a record's quantity: they compare as unchanged. |
  
  | `AmountProblem` | Number | The text | Examples |
  |---|---|---|---|
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_UNSPECIFIED` | 0 | is an amount. | `3.5 g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_EMPTY` | 1 | is nothing but whitespace. | an empty text |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_NO_NUMBER` | 2 | does not start with a number. A word in front makes it prose. | `g`, `THC 22%`, `up to 22 %`, `Blueberry Gummies 100mg` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_NO_UNIT` | 3 | is a number and nothing after it. In a range, the last end has to say the unit. | `3.5`, `18-22`, `1/8` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_AMBIGUOUS_SEPARATOR` | 4 | has a comma that is not grouping thousands, so it could be read two ways. | `3,5 g`, `1,00 mg`, `0,500 g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_BAD_NUMBER` | 5 | starts as a number and is not one. | `1.2.3 g`, `1/0 oz`, `1 3/2 oz` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_NEGATIVE` | 6 | is below zero. | `-1 g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_BACKWARDS_RANGE` | 7 | is a range that runs backwards. | `22-18 %` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_MIXED_UNITS` | 8 | is a range whose two ends say different units. | `500 mg - 1 g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_MULTIPLE` | 9 | is one amount times another. A hundred milligrams a package or ten an item: only the caller knows which its quantity means. | `10 x 10 mg`, `2 × 0.5 g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_QUALIFIED` | 10 | starts with `<`, `>`, `≤`, `≥`, `~`, `≈`, or `±`. "Less than one" is not a number and a unit. | `<1 %`, `~3.5 g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_UNREADABLE_UNIT` | 11 | has something after the number that is not made of what a unit is made of: letters, spaces, `%`, `.`, `/`, a hyphen between two letters, and a number only as the amount of what it is per. | `3.5 g (1/8 oz)`, `20 %+`, `5 mg 2`, `1e3 mg` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_TOO_LARGE` | 12 | is a number and a unit, and too large to compare. | a 1 followed by 400 zeros, in `g` |
  | `AMOUNT_PROBLEM_AMBIGUOUS_NAME` | 13 | is a word that names more than one weight. `half ounce` and `half gram` are read. | `half`, `a half` |
  
  | `Dimension` | Number | Meaning |
  |---|---|---|
  | `DIMENSION_UNSPECIFIED` | 0 | No unit to speak of: in a `Parse` answer, the text was not an amount. |
  | `DIMENSION_MASS` | 1 | A mass. Compared in `mg`. |
  | `DIMENSION_VOLUME` | 2 | A volume. Compared in `ml`. |
  | `DIMENSION_FRACTION` | 3 | A share of the whole. Compared in `%`. |
  | `DIMENSION_OTHER` | 4 | A unit this server does not convert: `pack`, `each`, a misspelling. It is its own dimension, the same as itself and different from every other unit. |
  
  ## Errors
  
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | `INVALID_ARGUMENT` | More than 1000 texts in one call. Send them in several. |
  
  A text that is not an amount is an answer with its reason, never an error: a caller checking a column wants every
  answer and a count of each reason.
}
